# 4. REQUIRED DESIGN QUALITY STANDARD

The final UI must meet the standard expected from:

- Senior frontend engineer
- Senior product designer
- Professional production website

The result must feel intentional.

Every visible design choice must answer at least one of:

- What is the primary user action?
- What is the information hierarchy?
- What semantic role does this color have?
- What component system does this element belong to?
- How does this behave on mobile?
- How does keyboard interaction work?

If an element has no clear answer, simplify or remove it.

---

# 5. MOBILE NAVBAR REMEDIATION

## 5.1 Required Header Structure

The mobile header MUST contain a clear, stable structure:

```text
[ Brand / Logo ]                      [ Search ] [ Menu ]
```

OR another structure justified by the design system.

Requirements:

- Single consistent header height.
- Brand must not be visually compressed.
- Touch targets must be comfortable.
- Controls must align vertically.
- No accidental wrapping.
- No clipping.
- No content should appear behind the header.

Recommended touch target:

- Minimum 44 × 44 CSS pixels for primary mobile controls.

---

## 5.2 Header Positioning

If the header is sticky:

- It MUST have a defined background.
- It MUST have an intentional z-index token.
- Page content MUST account for its occupied space.
- It MUST NOT allow the first page section to render underneath it unless intentionally designed.

Recommended design token concept:

```text
z-base
z-content
z-header
z-overlay
z-modal
```

Do NOT use arbitrary values such as random `z-[9999]` values throughout the codebase.

Centralize layering rules.

---

## 5.3 Mobile Menu Behavior

The mobile menu MUST be treated as an intentional navigation surface.

Choose one primary pattern based on UI UX Pro Max recommendations:

### Option A — Full-screen navigation sheet

or

### Option B — Side drawer

The final choice must be consistent.

The menu MUST include:

- Clear navigation hierarchy.
- Clear close action.
- Focus-visible states.
- Proper overlay/background treatment.
- Scroll behavior that does not break.
- No page content interaction through the menu.
- Clear active navigation state.

When the menu is open:

- Background scrolling should be intentionally handled.
- Menu content must not be clipped.
- The close button must remain accessible.
- The menu must not appear partially underneath the header.

---

## 5.4 Mobile Menu Animation

Animation is optional but, if used:

- Keep it short.
- Keep it functional.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.
- Do not animate layout in a way that causes visible jank.

The menu should feel responsive, not decorative.

---

## 5.5 Navbar Acceptance Criteria

PASS only when:

- Opening the menu does not create layout shifts.
- No content is clipped.
- No content appears unexpectedly under the header.
- Close action works.
- Navigation links are fully accessible.
- Keyboard focus is visible.
- Escape closes the menu if implemented as a dialog/sheet pattern.
- No horizontal overflow appears at 375px width.
- Header works consistently after scrolling.
- Mobile and desktop navigation share the same information architecture.

---

# 6. COLOR SYSTEM REMEDIATION

## 6.1 Core Principle

The website must use a limited semantic palette.

Do NOT assign colors based on individual preference per component.

Every color must have a role.

---

## 6.2 Required Semantic Roles

Define centralized tokens for:

```text
background
background-subtle
surface
surface-elevated

text-primary
text-secondary
text-muted

brand-primary
brand-primary-hover
brand-primary-active
brand-subtle

border-subtle
border-default
border-strong

focus-ring

success
warning
danger
info
```

The exact hex values must be selected through the design-system workflow and validated for contrast.

---

## 6.3 Product Color Direction

The product should communicate:

- Technical competence.
- Trust.
- Clarity.
- Professional service.

The preferred direction is a restrained technology palette with one strong brand accent.

Avoid:

- Rainbow UI.
- Multiple unrelated blues.
- Random accent colors.
- Gradient-first branding.
- Neon cyberpunk styling.
- Excessive saturated surfaces.

The brand accent should be used strategically for:

- Primary actions.
- Important interactive states.
- Key emphasis.
- Focus or selected states where appropriate.

The brand accent should NOT be used to color arbitrary text everywhere.

---

## 6.4 Heading Accent Rule

The current pattern of coloring part of a large heading MUST be reviewed.

Partial heading accent is allowed only when:

- It improves hierarchy.
- It remains readable.
- It does not become repetitive.
- It is consistent with the design system.

Do NOT force colored words into every heading.

---

## 6.5 Surface Hierarchy

The page must clearly distinguish:

1. Page background.
2. Content surface.
3. Elevated interactive surface.
4. Active/selected surface.

The difference must be visible without creating excessive contrast.

Avoid making the entire website look like a collection of disconnected white rectangles on gray backgrounds.

---

# 7. TYPOGRAPHY REMEDIATION

## 7.1 Requirements

Create a deliberate type scale.

At minimum define:

- Display / Hero
- H1
- H2
- H3
- Body Large
- Body
- Small
- Label
- Caption

Do not use arbitrary font sizes in individual components.

---

## 7.2 Hero Heading

The hero heading must:

- Have a clear line length.
- Wrap naturally on mobile.
- Avoid awkward single-word lines where possible.
- Use responsive sizing with controlled bounds.
- Maintain enough contrast.

Do NOT rely on oversized text that forces uncomfortable wrapping.

---

## 7.3 Body Copy

Body text must:

- Have sufficient contrast.
- Use comfortable line height.
- Avoid excessively wide line length on desktop.
- Remain readable on mobile.

---

# 8. SPACING AND LAYOUT SYSTEM

## 8.1 Mandatory Spacing Scale

Use a centralized spacing scale.

Do NOT use random values simply because they visually work in one component.

The scale may be based on a 4px or other coherent unit chosen by the design system.

---

## 8.2 Vertical Rhythm

Audit:

- Header to hero spacing.
- Hero internal spacing.
- Hero to search spacing.
- Search to supporting suggestions.
- Hero to next section.
- Section heading to section content.
- Card grid spacing.
- Footer spacing.

The page must feel connected rather than separated into arbitrary blocks.

---

## 8.3 Content Width

Define clear container widths.

Requirements:

- Readable text measure.
- Wide layouts do not stretch content excessively.
- Cards align to the same container logic.
- Header and main content follow intentional alignment rules.

---

# 9. BUTTON AND CTA SYSTEM

## 9.1 Required Variants

Define and use:

- Primary
- Secondary
- Tertiary / text
- Icon button

Do NOT invent new button styles per section.

---

## 9.2 Current CTA Review

The "Lihat Semua Layanan" style and search controls must be reviewed together.

Questions to resolve:

- Is the CTA visually primary or secondary?
- Does the border weight match other controls?
- Does the arrow/icon belong to the same icon system?
- Does the search button visually match the primary brand action?
- Are hover, active, and focus states defined?

---

## 9.3 Button Requirements

All buttons must have:

- Default state.
- Hover state.
- Active state.
- Focus-visible state.
- Disabled state when applicable.
- Consistent radius.
- Consistent padding.
- Accessible contrast.

---

# 10. SEARCH COMPONENT REMEDIATION

The search component is a key product feature and should feel like a product control, not a generic input.

Requirements:

- Clear search affordance.
- Comfortable mobile height.
- Input text never overlaps icons.
- Placeholder truncation handled gracefully.
- Submit button aligned and sized consistently.
- Focus state visible.
- No clipped content.
- Search suggestions remain readable.

The agent MUST test long placeholder text and long user input.

---

# 11. CARD SYSTEM REMEDIATION

Review all cards.

The website must avoid:

- Card overload.
- Unnecessary nested cards.
- Excessive shadows.
- Random border colors.

Cards should be used when they represent:

- A selectable service.
- A category.
- A grouped interactive unit.
- A meaningful elevated surface.

Section text does not automatically require a card.

---

# 12. ICON SYSTEM

Use one consistent SVG icon library or a consistent project icon system.

Do NOT mix:

- Emojis.
- Random Unicode symbols.
- Different icon stroke styles.
- Filled and outlined icons without rules.

Icons must:

- Align consistently.
- Have predictable size.
- Use semantic labels where required.

---

# 13. RESPONSIVE REMEDIATION TEST MATRIX

The agent MUST validate all major pages at:

```text
320px
375px
390px
768px
1024px
1280px
1440px
```

The additional small widths are mandatory because the current issue is mobile-navigation related.

Test:

## Header

- Brand fits.
- Controls fit.
- No overlap.
- No clipping.

## Hero

- Heading wraps correctly.
- Paragraph remains readable.
- CTA remains visible.
- Search fits.

## Navigation

- Menu opens fully.
- Menu closes fully.
- Scroll works.
- Overlay works.

## Cards

- Grid reflows.
- Text does not overflow.
- Touch targets remain usable.

---

# 14. ACCESSIBILITY REQUIREMENTS

The remediation must improve accessibility, not reduce it.

Verify:

- Semantic `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, and `<footer>`.
- Correct heading hierarchy.
- Buttons used for actions.
- Links used for navigation.
- `aria-expanded` for menu controls when applicable.
- `aria-controls` where appropriate.
- Focus management for modal/drawer patterns.
- Visible focus indicators.
- Contrast compliance.
- Keyboard navigation.

Do not remove focus rings merely because they are visually inconvenient.

Style them according to the design system.

---

# 15. IMPLEMENTATION PROCESS

The coding agent MUST execute in this order.

## Step 1 — Inspect Existing Implementation

Inspect:

- Header component.
- Mobile navigation component.
- Global CSS.
- Tailwind configuration/theme.
- Design system files.
- Layout component.
- Page wrappers.
- Z-index usage.
- Overflow rules.

Do not modify files before identifying the actual implementation.

---

## Step 2 — Root Cause Analysis

Document the root causes of:

1. Navbar bug.
2. Content clipping or overlap.
3. Color inconsistency.
4. Component inconsistency.

Do not guess.

Use actual code inspection.

---

## Step 3 — Update Design System

Update:

```text
design-system/MASTER.md
```

Document:

- Final color tokens.
- Header behavior.
- Layering/z-index strategy.
- Navigation pattern.
- Typography.
- Spacing.
- Button system.
- Search system.
- Card rules.

---

## Step 4 — Implement Shared Fixes First

Fix shared foundations before individual pages:

1. Global tokens.
2. Global layout.
3. Header.
4. Navigation.
5. Button.
6. Input/search.
7. Containers.

---

## Step 5 — Reconcile Pages

After shared fixes, inspect:

- Homepage.
- Services.
- Service detail.
- Categories.
- Search.
- How it works.
- Remote guide.
- FAQ.
- Informational pages.

Fix any page that violates the new design system.

---

## Step 6 — Visual QA

Review at all required viewport sizes.

Do not stop at "it compiles".

---

# 16. PROHIBITED QUICK FIXES

The agent MUST NOT solve the problem by:

- Adding arbitrary negative margins.
- Adding random `z-index: 9999`.
- Hiding overflowing content without understanding why.
- Using `overflow-x-hidden` to conceal a layout bug.
- Hardcoding one viewport-specific offset.
- Changing colors independently in multiple components.
- Adding more borders/shadows to compensate for weak hierarchy.
- Replacing the navbar with a random template.
- Rewriting the entire application unnecessarily.

Every fix must address the root cause.

---

